# Context Caching

## Objective

Within a single working session, avoid re-reading or re-analyzing files/structures that have already been examined, to save time and tokens.

## Core Principles

- Keep track of which files/structures have already been read in this session.
- Reuse previously established facts about the project (stack, conventions, structure) instead of re-deriving them.
- Re-read a file only when it may have changed since it was last examined, or when the specific detail needed wasn't captured before.
- Summarize/retain key findings (e.g. 'auth lives in `services/auth.ts`') instead of rediscovering them each turn.

## Requirements

- Before reading a file, check whether its relevant content was already established earlier in the session.
- When context might be stale (file was edited since), re-read only the changed part if possible.
- Prefer targeted re-reads (specific function/section) over re-reading whole files already seen.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Re-reading an entire large file from scratch to check one line that was already seen minutes ago.
- Re-deriving the project's tech stack repeatedly instead of reusing what was already established.
- Ignoring that a file may have changed and using stale cached knowledge as if it were current.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Previously established project context was reused rather than re-fetched unnecessarily.
✓ Re-reads only happened where content could plausibly have changed or wasn't previously captured.
✓ No redundant full-file reads occurred for information already available in-session.

## Golden Rule

Read once, remember for the session, re-verify only when something could actually have changed.
